===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪ.d͡ʒɪn/|a=UK}}
* {{enPR|pĭjʹən|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪ.d͡ʒən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-pigeon.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪd͡ʒən|ɪd͡ʒɪn|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|pidgin}}

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|pygeoun}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|pyjon}}, inherited from {{der|en|LL.|pipio|pīpiōnem|t=chirping bird}}, derived from {{der|en|la|pipio|pīpiāre|t=chirp}}, of imitative origin.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|pidgeon}} {{q|chiefly archaic}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# One of several birds of the family {{taxfmt|Columbidae|family}}, which consists of more than 300 species.
#: {{syn|en|columbid|culver|dove}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[meat]] from this bird.
# {{lb|en|North America|informal}} A person who is a [[target]] or [[victim]] of a [[confidence game]].
#: {{syn|en|dupe|fish|sucker|Thesaurus:dupe}}
# {{lb|en|countable|politics}} A [[pacifist]], [[appeaser]], an [[isolationist]], a [[dove]].
# A person hired to [[transport]] [[film]] [[footage]] out of a region where transport options are limited.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|author=Whitman Bassow|title=The Moscow Correspondents|page=214
|passage=Kalb rushed to the airport and found a "'''pigeon'''" to take out the film: an American woman headed for London.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2021|author=Hilary Brown|title=War Tourist: Memoirs of a Foreign Correspondent
|passage=At this point, all the commercial airports in Pakistan were closed. The only way to get film out was over land. John promptly hired me to be what was then known in the business as a "'''Pigeon'''," and installed me in a comfortable room in his hotel. {{...}} I would then hand-carry his film out of the country, via Peshawar, the Khyber Pass, through the Kabul Gorge, and up to Kabul, Afghanistan, where I would meet a BBC courier and transfer the film bag.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|[[military]] [[slang]]}} A weak or useless person.

